 "ocr_text": "momcozy\nmomcozy\nmomcozy\nNo fever. Still congested.\nDo I take them in?\nIt's 2 AM. You're staring at the crib. They sound\nawful. The thermometer says they're fine.\nYou're torn: \"Do I risk the waiting room germs,\nor am I waiting too long?\"\nFever is only half the story.\nA normal thermometer doesn't always mean\n\"all clear.\" But it's not a reason to panic either.\nWatch how they're breathing and acting -\nnot just the number.\nSource: AAP HealthyChildren (healthychildren.org / Children & Colds)\nHow long is too long?\nmomcozy\nBREATHE EASY.\nWatch at home if:\nMost infant colds fade in 7-10 days\n(though congestion can linger).\nWatch For\nThe Safe Sign\nEating, sleeping, and smiling between sniffles.\n4-6+ heavy wet diapers in 24 hours.\nRelaxed and steady while asleep (no gasping).\nWatch For\nSwipe →\nDay1\nOnset\nDay 7-10\nDay14\nMost Common Colds Improve\nStill Not Better?\nCall the doctor if:\n• Symptoms last 10-14+ days without improving.\n• They get better, then suddenly get worse.\nThe Bottom Line\nTreat the baby, not the thermometer. You know them best.\nSwipe →\nSource: AAD HealthvChildren / Penn Medicine / Sanford Health\nmomcozy\nDON'T WAIT.\nCall the doctor if:\nThe Red Flag\nUnder 3 months old with any cold signs.\nWorking hard: ribs sucking in, nostrils\nflaring, or breathing fast.\nBlue-ish tint on lips or nail beds.\nNo wet diaper in 8+ hours, dry lips, or crying\nwith no tears.\nHome Relief Checklist:\nSaline drops\nCool-mist humidifier\n• Upright snuggles (awake only)\nRefusing all feeds or very hard to wake up.\nSource: AAD HealthvChildren. Penn Medicine. Sanford Health: AAD Safe Sleen Guideline‹\nSwipe →\nSource: AAD Healthychildren \"Chicren & Colde\" Seattle Children'e \"Coles 0-72 Months\nSwipe",
